{{also|double-whammy}}
==English==
{{was wotd|2022|August|1}}

===Etymology===
From {{compound|en|double|whammy|t2=evil spell; curse or hex|notext=1|type=endocentric}}, popularized by the [[American#Adjective|American]] [[cartoonist]] {{w|Al Capp}} (1909–1979), in his classic [[comic strip]] ''[[w:Li'l Abner|Li’l Abner]]'' (1934–1977).<ref>{{R:OED Online|subentry=a double whammy|subid=14672753|entry=whammy|pos=n|id=228047|date=December 2021|nodot=1}}; {{R:Lexico|pos=n}}</ref>

===Pronunciation===
* {{a|RP}} {{IPA|en|/ˌdʌbl̩ ˈ(h)wæmi/}}
* {{a|GA}} {{IPA|en|/ˌdʌb(ə)l ˈ(h)wæmi/}}
* {{audio|en|En-us-double whammy.oga|Audio (GA)}}
* {{rhymes|en|æmi|s=4}}
* {{hyphenation|en|doub|le wham|my}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun}} {{term-label|en|informal}}

# A [[series]] of [[two#Numeral|two]] [[event#Noun|events]] that [[cause#Verb|causes]] [[adverse]] [[effect#Noun|effects]]; a [[twofold#Adjective|twofold]] [[blow#Noun|blow]] or [[setback]].
#* {{quote-journal|en|author=Robert Kitson|title=[[w:England national rugby union team|England]] into World Cup semi-finals after bruising victory over [[w:Australia national rugby union team|Australia]]|editor={{w|Katharine Viner}}|newspaper={{w|The Guardian}}|archiveurl=https://web.archive.org/web/20220722213003/https://www.theguardian.com/sport/2019/oct/19/england-australia-rugby-world-cup-match-report|archivedate=22 July 2022|location=London|publisher=[[w:Guardian Media Group|Guardian News & Media]]|date=19 October 2019|issn=0261-3077|oclc=229952407|passage=Australia had looked the brighter side initially, probing for gaps in England’s defensive spacing and taking an early lead via [[w:Christian Leali'ifano|Christian Lealiifano]]'s first successful penalty. Their opponents took a while to make any kind of consistent front-foot impression before [[w:Jonny May|[Jonny] May]]'s '''double whammy''' inside four minutes banished their jangling nerves.}}
# {{lb|en|by extension}} A series of two events that causes [[positive#Adjective|positive]] effects; a twofold [[boon#Noun|boon]].

